
# 2026-03-19 20:23:19.559970
+BUG DESCRIPTION --------------- Actual Behavior: GET /items/{id} raises a KeyError when the item does not exist and causes a server crash; GET /items/{id}/total raises a ZeroDivisionError when item quantity is 0 causing a 500 crash; DELETE /items/{id} can raise a KeyError for missing items and on success returns no clear response body or proper status. Expected Behavior: GET /items/{id} should return 404 Not Found with a JSON error when the item is missing; GET /items/{id}/total should validate quantity and return 400 Bad Request with a JSON error when quantity is 0 instead of crashing; DELETE /items/{id} should return 204 No Content on successful deletion and 404 Not Found when the item does not exist, with no unhandled exceptions. Affected Area: Items API endpoints (GET /items/{id}, GET /items/{id}/total, DELETE /items/{id}), item retrieval and delete handlers, and total calculation logic. ASSUMPTIONS ----------- - ⚠️ The backend is implemented in Python (KeyError and ZeroDivisionError indicate Python) and currently lacks proper existence and input validation. - ⚠️ Error handling middleware or explicit try/except blocks are missing or not translating exceptions to HTTP responses for these endpoints. ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA ------------------- 1. Given a non-existent item id, when calling GET /items/{id}, then the API returns 404 Not Found with a JSON body containing a single 'error' string and no unhandled exception is raised. 2. Given an item with quantity 0, when calling GET /items/{id}/total, then the API returns 400 Bad Request with a JSON body containing a descriptive 'error' message like 'quantity must be greater than 0' and no ZeroDivisionError occurs. 3. No regression in related functionality: given an existing item, when DELETE /items/{id} is called, then the API returns 204 No Content and the item is removed; given a non-existent id, DELETE returns 404 Not Found, and no unhandled exceptions are raised.
+
+  Instructions:
+  - Read the repo map and find the relevant files yourself.
+  - Make the minimal, targeted change needed.
+  - Do not refactor unrelated code.
+  - Do not create new files unless necessary.
+  

